/** @page avroschemaguide avro Schema and Type Guide

    @tableofcontents

    @section avroschemaguidetypes Type Mapping

    |!Avro Type|!Decodes To|!Encodes From
    |\c null|@ref nothing|@ref nothing or @ref null
    |\c boolean|\c bool|\c bool
    |\c int|\c int|\c int in the range [-2^31, 2^31)
    |\c long|\c int|\c int
    |\c float|\c float|\c float, \c int or \c number
    |\c double|\c float|\c float, \c int or \c number
    |\c bytes|\c binary|\c binary, or a \c string's bytes
    |\c string|\c string (UTF-8)|\c string
    |\c record|\c hash|\c hash
    |\c enum|\c string (the symbol)|a \c string symbol or an \c int index
    |\c array|\c list|\c list
    |\c map|\c hash|\c hash
    |\c union|the branch's value|see @ref avroschemaguideunion
    |\c fixed|\c binary of exactly \c size bytes|\c binary of exactly \c size bytes

    Decoding is strict: an \c int that does not fit in 32 bits, a \c string that is not valid
    UTF-8, an out-of-range \c enum index or union branch index, a negative length and a truncated
    value all raise \c AVRO-DECODE-ERROR rather than producing a corrupt value.

    @section avroschemaguidelogical Logical Types

    |!Logical Type|!Base Type|!Qore Value
    |\c decimal|\c bytes or \c fixed|\c number, converted exactly; see @ref avroschemaguidedecimal
    |\c uuid|\c string, or \c fixed[16]|\c string, or \c binary over \c fixed[16]
    |\c date|\c int (days since 1970-01-01)|absolute \c date at UTC midnight
    |\c time-millis|\c int (ms since midnight)|<b>relative</b> \c date (a duration)
    |\c time-micros|\c long (µs since midnight)|<b>relative</b> \c date (a duration)
    |\c timestamp-millis|\c long (ms since the epoch)|absolute \c date in UTC
    |\c timestamp-micros|\c long (µs since the epoch)|absolute \c date in UTC
    |\c local-timestamp-millis|\c long|absolute \c date in the <b>local</b> zone
    |\c local-timestamp-micros|\c long|absolute \c date in the <b>local</b> zone
    |\c duration|\c fixed[12] (3 × little-endian \c uint32)|relative \c date

    \c time-millis and \c time-micros are a time of day with no date attached, and %Qore has no
    time-only type; a relative date (\c PT13H45M30S) says exactly that and nothing more, where an
    absolute date on 1970-01-01 would invent a date the data do not contain.

    \c local-timestamp-millis and \c local-timestamp-micros are wall-clock readings with no zone.
    %Qore dates always carry a zone, so they are materialised in the local zone — the only reading
    under which formatting the value reproduces the written wall-clock time.

    Encoding raises \c AVRO-ENCODE-ERROR rather than rounding: a value with microsecond precision
    cannot be written to a millisecond logical type, and an absolute date cannot be written to
    \c time-millis (or a relative one to \c timestamp-micros).

    @note the specification requires an implementation to ignore a logical type it does not
    recognise, or one that is invalid for its base type — a \c decimal whose \c scale exceeds its
    \c precision, for instance — and fall back to the base type. This module does that;
    @ref Qore::Avro::AvroSchema::getLogicalType() "getLogicalType()" reports what was actually
    applied, not what the schema text says.

    @section avroschemaguidedecimal Decimal

    An Avro \c decimal is a \c bytes or \c fixed value holding a two's-complement big-endian
    <i>unscaled integer</i>, with \c precision and \c scale in the schema. Precision commonly
    exceeds 18 digits, so the unscaled value does not fit in a 64-bit integer; every conversion is
    therefore routed through an exact decimal string, and \c decimal(38, 2) and beyond round-trip
    exactly.

    @code{.py}
AvroSchema schema('{"type": "bytes", "logicalType": "decimal", "precision": 38, "scale": 10}');
binary b = schema.encode(12345678901234567890.1234567890n);
printf("%y\n", schema.decode(b));
    @endcode

    Encoding accepts a \c number, \c int, \c float or \c string. Nothing is silently rounded: a
    value with more fractional digits than \c scale, or more significant digits than
    \c precision, raises \c AVRO-ENCODE-ERROR.

    @note %Qore's \c number is an arbitrary-precision <i>binary</i> floating-point type whose
    precision is derived from the text it was built from, so two \c number values with identical
    decimal representations can still compare unequal with \c == if they were built from strings
    of different lengths — this is true of \c number generally and is not specific to Avro. The
    guarantee this module makes is that the decoded value's decimal representation is identical
    to the encoded one; compare decimals with \c toString() when an exact test is needed.

    @section avroschemaguideunion Union Branch Selection

    Decoding a union is unambiguous — the branch index is on the wire. Encoding is not: a %Qore
    string offered to <tt>["string", "bytes"]</tt> could go either way. The module resolves this
    with a fixed preference order rather than requiring callers to wrap values:

    |!Qore Value|!Branch Preference
    |@ref nothing / @ref null|\c null
    |\c bool|\c boolean
    |\c int|a \c decimal branch, then \c int (if the value fits in 32 bits), \c long, \c float, \c double
    |\c float|a \c decimal branch, then \c double, \c float
    |\c number|a \c decimal branch, then \c double, \c float
    |\c string|\c enum (if the value is one of its symbols), then \c string, \c bytes
    |\c binary|\c bytes, then \c fixed of exactly the right size
    |absolute \c date|\c timestamp-micros, \c timestamp-millis, \c local-timestamp-micros, \
        \c local-timestamp-millis, \c date
    |relative \c date|\c duration, \c time-micros, \c time-millis
    |\c list|\c array
    |\c hash|the \c record branch it satisfies with the fewest unmatched keys, then \c map

    Within a branch kind the first matching branch in schema order wins. If no branch accepts the
    value, \c AVRO-ENCODE-ERROR is raised naming the value's type and every branch of the union.

    The common <tt>["null", T]</tt> shape therefore needs no special handling at all:

    @code{.py}
AvroSchema schema(("null", "string"));
schema.encode(NOTHING);     # the "null" branch
schema.encode("a value");   # the "string" branch
    @endcode

    @section avroschemaguideresolution Reader/Writer Schema Resolution

    Pass the writer's schema to @ref Qore::Avro::AvroSchema::decode() "decode()" to read data
    written with a different — usually older — version of the schema. The object the method is
    called on is the <i>reader</i> schema, and the result matches it:

    @code{.py}
hash<auto> v = reader_schema.decode(data, writer_schema);
    @endcode

    The specification's reconciliation rules are applied in full:

    |!Situation|!Behaviour
    |a writer field the reader does not declare|skipped without materialising a value
    |a reader field the writer did not write|filled from the reader field's \c default; \
        \c AVRO-RESOLUTION-ERROR if it has none
    |fields in a different order|matched by name, and returned in <b>reader</b> order
    |a renamed field|matched if the reader's field declares the writer's name in its \c aliases
    |a renamed named type|matched by fullname, by a reader alias, or by unqualified name
    |a writer enum symbol the reader does not declare|replaced by the reader enum's \c default \
        symbol; \c AVRO-RESOLUTION-ERROR if it has none
    |a widened numeric type|\c int → \c long / \c float / \c double, \c long → \c float / \
        \c double, and \c float → \c double are all read
    |\c string and \c bytes|interchangeable in both directions
    |the writer's schema is a union|the branch on the wire is resolved against the reader's schema
    |the reader's schema is a union|the first branch that matches the writer's schema is used

    @section avroschemaguidecanonical Canonical Form and Fingerprints

    @ref Qore::Avro::AvroSchema::getCanonicalForm() "getCanonicalForm()" returns the
    <a href="https://avro.apache.org/docs/current/specification/#parsing-canonical-form-for-schemas">Parsing
    Canonical Form</a>: everything that does not affect how data are read — documentation,
    aliases, logical types, default values, attribute order and whitespace — is stripped, and
    every name is resolved to its fullname.

    @ref Qore::Avro::AvroSchema::getFingerprint() "getFingerprint()" returns the CRC-64-AVRO
    (64-bit Rabin) fingerprint of that form, as a signed integer, matching the specification's
    reference implementation. Use it as a cache key when the transport supplies no stable schema
    ID.

    @section avroschemaguidenamed Named Types

    Records, enums and fixed types have names, and a name may be referenced anywhere later in the
    schema — including from inside the type itself, which is how recursive structures such as
    linked lists and trees are described:

    @code{.py}
AvroSchema schema('{
    "type": "record",
    "name": "Node",
    "fields": [
        {"name": "value", "type": "int"},
        {"name": "next", "type": ["null", "Node"], "default": null}
    ]
}');
    @endcode

    @ref Qore::Avro::AvroSchema::getNamedTypeNames() "getNamedTypeNames()" lists every named type
    in definition order, and @ref Qore::Avro::AvroSchema::getNamedType() "getNamedType()" returns
    a schema handle rooted at one of them. The handle shares the parsed schema, so it is cheap and
    keeps the parent alive for as long as it exists.

    @note a datum, not just a schema, can drive recursion depth through a recursive type, so the
    256-level nesting limit is enforced in all builds and is not a sandboxing option
*/
